  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">EASTERN ELEPHANTS ASSESSING THE POPULATION STATUS, DISTRIBUTION AND CONSERVATION THREATS TO THE FRAGMENTED ASIAN ELEPHANT POPULATION IN INDIA S EASTERN GHATS</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE ASIAN ELEPHANT IS THE LARGEST TERRESTRIAL MAMMAL IN TROPICAL ASIA AND A FLAGSHIP SPECIES FOR REGIONAL CONSERVATION EFFORTS ACROSS INDIA AND SOUTH ASIA. HISTORICALLY, ASIAN ELEPHANTS WERE DISTRIBUTED OVER AN AREA EXCEEDING 9 MILLION KM2 ACROSS ASIA, BUT THEIR RANGE HAS SINCE REDUCED BY OVER 95%. THIS LOSS OF ELEPHANT RANGE IS EVEN MORE PRONOUNCED IN INDIA, WHICH HOLDS THE LARGEST POPULATION OF THE SPECIES WITH AN ESTIMATED 30,000 WILD ELEPHANTS. DEFORESTATION AND LOSS OF HABITAT, HUNTING AND WILDLIFE TRADE FOR IVORY, CONFLICT WITH PEOPLE, IS SEVERELY THREATENING THEIR POPULATIONS ACROSS INDIA. WHILE A SIGNIFICANT AMOUNT OF RESEARCH HAS BEEN DONE ON ELEPHANT POPULATIONS ACROSS THE WESTERN GHATS, NORTHEAST INDIA AND THE HIMALAYAS, THERE ARE OTHER SMALLER AND SCATTERED POPULATIONS OF ELEPHANTS SPREAD ACROSS INDIA WHERE LITTLE RESEARCH OR CONSERVATION EFFORTS HAVE TAKEN PLACE. ONE OF THE MOST IMPORTANT OF THESE IS INDIAS EASTERN GHATS MOUNTAIN LANDSCAPE, RUNNING PARALLEL TO INDIAS EAST COAST FOR A DISTANCE OF 1600 KM NORTH TO SOUTH COVERING 75,000 KM2 AND CONTAINING 12 PROTECTED AREAS. IT IS ALSO THE LEAST PROTECTED FOREST LANDSCAPE IN INDIA, WITH ONLY 6.3% OF TOTAL FOREST AREA UNDER PROTECTED AREA COVERAGE. CURRENTLY, THE FORESTS OF THE EASTERN GHATS ARE EXPERIENCING RAPID DEFORESTATION DUE TO MINING, AGRICULTUR</narrative>
  </description>
